#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171774 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e7e0a0-9388-5346-ab96-f2dec237c9f5" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "231de5e4-8c55-5129-baaf-57673050ddbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8505" :question/problem "For the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y$,\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,2)$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1)$, $(0,1)$, and $(1,2)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(1,1)}&=2(1)^2-1=1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(0,1)}&=2(0)^2-1=-1,\\\\\n\\left.y'\\right|_{(1,2)}&=2(1)^2-2=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/2e6c81d3cc119edb8bc6c9a5813acfef.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171775 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0444de80-19f1-5251-b159-5dc55756bb19" :question/math-academy-id "e-8506" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n2. At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n3. At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we define\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = -2xy\n$$\n\nThen, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 0\n$$\n\nwhen $x=0$ or $y=0$. The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/f5f03b1c9ed7aee9818764ccc0c59265.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y) \\lt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171776 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3602a14d-6bd7-5586-a6e8-51d069dc8641" :question/math-academy-id "e-8507" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/93bde689614608eb91d38a348bf71381.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y$. This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/a5252d432d85bf6081707a2e6a174d66.png)\n\nTo verify the selected field specifically for $y'=x+y$, notice that its segments are horizontal on $y=-x$, positive above that line, and negative below it. For example, the slopes at $(1,0)$ and $(0,-1)$ are $1$ and $-1$, respectively. These checks agree with the selected diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/70f3eb2943cb191f8644c5f157ecc858.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171777 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab9fe3df-7f60-55c5-b8af-810b0746993e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8576" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n2. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n3. At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we define\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1)\n$$\n\nThen, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 0\n$$\n\nwhen $x = -1$ or $y = -1$. This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/0f8a9ed747232a1b4e5c6ff0861af3cd.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/f0f41e3b1b7a1732c9b95aac285ec4f4.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\gt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1$, then according to our diagram, we have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y) \\lt 0$.\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be637cc9-7c15-5846-9b5a-59cd2ded00d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/q-96476-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171362} {:answer/id #uuid "ed726290-ceb5-5726-8afe-1ea17d16e266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/q-96476-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171363} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc44594-a71f-5b20-b57b-18092760bac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/q-96476-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171364} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb644ed-508e-5f2e-8fad-99f9b98ae7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/q-96476-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171365} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe2168-b575-5e41-9930-a21f9258cef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/q-96476-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed726290-ceb5-5726-8afe-1ea17d16e266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3352/Source/Images/q-96476-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171363} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb4db775-85f9-5606-9329-b88efd180f72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e4e2c73-61a2-51eb-9aeb-b173fc18ef10" :question/math-academy-id "q-96476" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = xy$?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "For $y'=xy$, slopes are zero on both coordinate axes. They are positive when $x$ and $y$ have the same sign and negative when their signs differ. The selected field has horizontal segments on both axes, positive slopes in quadrants I and III, and negative slopes in II and IV; the magnitude grows with $|xy|$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5267c89-53ba-5bbe-ac45-899450d8b526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171416} {:answer/id #uuid "f0286959-2694-5759-911d-288e8aa46c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171417} {:answer/id #uuid "04f6f24d-b532-52ec-839a-51883f2f7753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171418} {:answer/id #uuid "182bdb06-70d7-5c74-8f7f-5b9dea9bf2e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171419} {:answer/id #uuid "43d27ff6-1df6-5e1f-ab4f-006dbaee7b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0286959-2694-5759-911d-288e8aa46c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171417} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a8a9fd-a206-571b-8ba0-8140f258b4d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e86fafab-049b-51e4-93da-aada8761c377" :question/math-academy-id "q-97538" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x + 1)^{2}(y - 2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < - 1$ and $y > 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n2. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < - 1$ and $y < 2$, the slope of the slope field is negative\n3. At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > - 1$ and $y < 2$, the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "In every listed region, $x\\ne-1$, so $(x+1)^2>0$. The slope therefore has the sign of $y-2$. It is positive above $y=2$ and negative below, on either side of $x=-1$. All three statements are true: select **I, II, and III**."}]}
